WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.850
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:08.640
My name is Kelly.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.060
I teach statistics
and data science.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.700
And on this first slide, you
are seeing the beautiful city

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.180
of Barcelona, Spain.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.480
And I put that up there
because a week from today,

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.810
I will hopefully be there
to meet my boyfriend's

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.030
parents for the first time.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.310
He is fully Spanish.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.370
I very wisely took
10 years of French,

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.640
so I don't really
speak Spanish and I

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.800
have been practicing a bit.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.540
And so that has
gotten me thinking

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.440
a little about translating
between ordinary languages

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.440
and translating between
programming languages.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.610
And so this whole
hook was, of course,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.610
just an excuse for me to
be like, hey, everyone,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.870
I have a boyfriend.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.420
That's him.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.240
And so, you know, I've been
listening to this podcast that

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.820
teaches Spanish, and
there was this phrase

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.070
that they were teaching
that kind of struck me.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.260
And it looked like this
"Me gusta ver a mis amigos".

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.980
Sorry for the pronunciation.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.660
And that there are these amigos
was the point of the episode.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.460
It's kind of a
weird construction

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.450
for what they're trying to say
is "I like to see my friends".

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.580
But of course, if you were
to translate, you know,

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.920
word for word or
literally to english,

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.350
we would be saying
"it is pleasing

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.110
to me to see to my friends",
which of course, is not

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.300
a thing that we say in English.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.690
And so this got me thinking
about that idea of a 1-to-1

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.610
direct translation and
how that's not really

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.920
how languages actually work.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.290
So can we apply
this sort of theory

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:42.680
of language learning to
programming languages instead?

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.390
So I'd like to start
off with a pop quiz.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.400
It's the first talk
of this year's rstudio::conf

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.310
besides the keynote.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.643
And I'm hitting you with a quiz.

00:01:48.643 --> 00:01:50.270
Sorry not sorry.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.850
Here is your pop quiz.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.740
Oops I don't know why
that's not showing you

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.720
my pop quiz in words.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.960
This code right here.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.270
Is the object
returned by that code

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.460
going to be two
dimensional, like a matrix

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.010
or a dataframe, or something
with rows and columns?

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.570
Or is it going to
be one dimensional,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.150
like a vector or an array?

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.920
Who's going to say
one dimensional?

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.795
Who's going to say
two dimensional?

00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:17.170
Could I see you
people who raised

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.295
your hands for two dimensional
after seeing the one

00:02:19.295 --> 00:02:20.230
dimensional?

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.470
The answer, as you might expect,
is it's a trick question.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.770
As we know, posit is no
longer an R only company.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.270
In R, this exact
code right here,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.720
taking a column of
the penguin's data set

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.070
is going to be one
dimensional, right?

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.420
That's yanking out
a column. In Python,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:38.720
that thing is two dimensional.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.370
That is a dataframe
with one column.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.440
So this is an example
of there is not

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.320
a direct translation between
these two rather similar,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.840
rather close languages.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.060
That exact code will
run in both languages

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.840
and it will produce a
different structure of results.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.383
So what motivated this?

00:02:57.383 --> 00:02:59.550
There's a class that I teach
every year at Cal Poly.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.750
It's called
statistical learning,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.780
and we're kind of upgrading it
to Statistical Learning with R,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.910
but then I've added
in the Python,

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.680
so this class is kind
of upper level class.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.190
It's mostly but not
entirely stat majors

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.680
in their final years of
their undergrad degree.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.490
There's no computing
prerequisite.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.640
I think going to change that.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.680
Turning this off because
I'm getting echo there.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.310
There's no computing
prerequisite.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.190
So any R or Python
that I use, I have

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.460
to kind of spoon
feed a little bit.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.710
Back when this class
was first offered in 2020,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:31.820
I didn't teach it.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.800
It was kind of a
hodgepodge, as many of us

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.050
have done with modeling.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:39.020
It was using caret and base R,
and tidyverse for the data

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.080
wrangling, and just kind
of all over the place.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.740
So when I took it over, I
changed it to tidymodels,

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:46.860
and that was really fun.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.230
Like tindmodels is
kind of new and it made

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.500
it so much easier to teach it.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.060
I could go on, but that's
not the point of this talk.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.740
And then this year,
for some reason,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.448
instead of doing the
smart thing and just

00:03:56.448 --> 00:03:59.480
reusing my materials with little
tweaks that had gone very well,

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.430
I decided to translate
everything also

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.050
into scikitlearn in Python.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.430
So I want to tell you
what this talk isn't.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.310
I am not encouraging
you to do this.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.220
This is extra work for
me without a huge amount

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.050
of benefit in the class.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.550
I am not trying to encourage
you to teach a bilingual class,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.133
although if you're
interested, I would

00:04:18.133 --> 00:04:19.390
love to talk to you about it.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.450
I did it kind of because there's
sort of these two funnels

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:23.233
into this course.

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:25.400
And one side of that funnel
is the computer science,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.650
data science minors.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.020
I wanted it to be accessible.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.510
I want it to fit in
our eventual data

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.790
science bachelor's program.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.210
And I really wanted to practice.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.610
And, you know, it's one of
those things where, like, you

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.235
commit when you're
feeling enthusiastic

00:04:38.235 --> 00:04:39.820
and then you're
forced into it, right?

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.195
So I wanted to
practice python, I

00:04:41.195 --> 00:04:44.002
wanted to pick up Quarto,
which is nice for using both.

00:04:44.002 --> 00:04:44.960
So that's why I did it.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.293
So I'm not telling you to do it.

00:04:46.293 --> 00:04:48.100
I did it for fun.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.725
Fun, so that's not the
point of this talk.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.290
So what is the
point of this talk?

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:58.820
What I want to talk
to you about is

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.100
what I learned from watching
the students learn differently

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.230
from these two languages,
like the two cohorts who

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.970
chose are versus Python.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.723
And specifically
thinking about how

00:05:07.723 --> 00:05:10.140
these different implementations,
not just across languages

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.810
but maybe even across
packages, is going to impact

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.810
understanding for these users.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.940
And specifically, I know
most people in this room,

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.740
maybe because of the
track, might be educators,

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.115
but I kind of have
split this out

00:05:21.115 --> 00:05:24.090
into advice for
developers or creators,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.820
for users of the tools,
and then, of course, most

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.923
importantly, for educators.

00:05:29.923 --> 00:05:30.590
One step behind.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:32.570
There we go.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.620
So let's dive in.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.800
So thinking about how
you learn a new language

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.510
or how you translate
between languages

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.940
and now I'm thinking back
to Spanish and French.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.290
Probably the first thing that
comes to mind is that's hard.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.410
That was in my first
example is grammar

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:48.190
and specifically like
sentence structure.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:51.060
Like why do these same words
go in a totally different order

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.490
in different languages?

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.440
And so how I might
kind of parallel

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.110
that in programming
languages is this idea

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.950
of what's the structure
of your analysis.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:02.867
Like you have to do these
multiple steps to do

00:06:02.867 --> 00:06:05.650
an analysis, but in
different implementations,

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:09.040
those steps might happen
at different times.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.250
So here's what I mean.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.440
Here's two code chunks.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.800
The top one is
scikitlearn / Python.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.550
The bottom one down there is
tidymodels / R, of course.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.810
And my question to you is,
when in these two chunks

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.030
do we decide which
predictors are

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.420
going to go into our models?

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.530
It's just the linear model
with a couple of predictors.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:31.890
And that's happening right here.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.330
So it feels kind of the same
in both languages right now,

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.830
right at the moment where you
are fitting the data after you

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.140
specified your
model, that's when

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:43.190
you're declaring which variables
are part of this model.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.480
But then you start thinking
about preprocessing.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.300
So here's some Python
code that includes

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.750
a step where we are
log transforming both

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.170
of our quantitative predictors.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.310
And so we added some
stuff to the process.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.990
But you can see in Python,
the preprocessing decision

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.330
happens early, and
then the decision

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.190
about which predictors
we're actually doing

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.030
that to happens at the fit step.

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:13.510
But in tidymodels here, 
we now

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:17.440
have this idea of 
recipes. this just

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:18.020
right?

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.090
So the recipe then
contains both the decision

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.970
about your predictors
and the decision

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:26.990
about how you are going
to transform them.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.000
And even though
it's all together,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.500
it's actually in
the opposite order.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.920
You choose your predictors
and then your transformations.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.670
And so this is
slightly different.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:35.960
And I'm not saying it's wrong.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.350
Like I don't think that either
one is necessarily wrong,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:42.800
but having different approaches
is going to have consequences.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.900
And so here's what
happened with my students,

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.930
like the ones that were using
tidymodels kind of focused

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.740
really on feature selection.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.710
They really focused
on, which predictors

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.130
are going to be in my model.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.430
And then once they decided
that they were like, yeah,

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.590
maybe we could log
transform them.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.723
And the ones who use scikit
learn really focused on how

00:08:00.723 --> 00:08:01.640
do we adjust the data?

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:02.960
They kind of thought
of this transformation

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.670
as part of the data
cleaning, and they

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:08.330
did that first, and then allowed
some predictors or others

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.960
to be in their model.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.480
So what's the takeaway
from that observation?

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.130
While developers,
I just want you

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:17.710
to be aware of how
your API design is

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.870
going to influence
how people think

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.880
about the actual concepts.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.410
This is not a separate thing.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.320
And then.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.130
I want, when possible,

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.160
if you can be kind
of agnostic, if you

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.720
can allow, like I'm thinking of
how the recipes and the model

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.220
specification in
tidymodels, it

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.000
doesn't really matter which
order you declare them.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.520
They're sort of
separate entities

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.250
that you put together later.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.720
I think that's a really good
design choice, personally.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.760
And then my last
suggestion is just

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.840
keeping kind of related
decisions together.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.080
Like I don't necessarily
think I scikitlearn is wrong,

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.747
but I don't like this thing that
you go through the whole setup.

00:08:55.747 --> 00:08:58.692
And then you specify
your predictors.

00:08:58.692 --> 00:09:00.400
And of course you
could like insert steps

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.920
where you make another
dataframe to solve for that.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.220
But, the way that it's
sort of naturally set up

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:10.160
doesn't keep those
decisions together.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.030
My advice for users here.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.360
Try to match your code
flow with your narrative

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.878
where you have choices.

00:09:15.878 --> 00:09:18.170
If you are thinking through
a problem in a certain way,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.210
try to match your code.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.950
And then, of course,
intersperse your code with text.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:26.780
This is why that discussion and
documentation is so important.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.520
If you're doing two
parallel analyses

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.020
in different
languages, but you have

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:32.828
to kind of shuffle
parts of it, then

00:09:32.828 --> 00:09:34.370
you are going to
need that discussion

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.755
text to help match those up.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.150
You know, and then
as much as you

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.785
can kind of compartmentalize,
make your steps modular.

00:09:42.785 --> 00:09:44.160
Here's where we
choose the model.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:45.910
Here's where we choose
the predictors that

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:50.870
also kind of lets you
swap between languages.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.550
And then my advice for
educators here, enforce

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.550
a workflow on your students
that matches the way

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.390
that you teach it.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:09:59.650
Two clicks.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:00.710
Break your analysis up.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:02.450
Same idea as the practitioners.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.120
If you are isolating
these decisions,

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.953
then when they see them
in different orders,

00:10:05.953 --> 00:10:08.850
they recognize that decision.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.220
And then really push
students to document,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.590
to write what they're doing,
to state their decisions

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.080
outside the context of the code.

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:18.480
All right.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:21.050
Next thing that I find
difficult in learning languages

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.950
is false friends or foes of me.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.560
When I learned French words
that sound really similar.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.060
And have different meanings.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:31.440
So if I come up to you later in
this conference and I say this.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.720
You're probably going to
make that face if you are not

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:34.653
a Spanish speaker.

00:10:34.653 --> 00:10:36.570
But it turns out I have
learned that this just

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:37.630
means I have a cold.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:38.820
I'm kind of congested.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.570
It doesn't mean what
you might think.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:42.363
Or another one.

00:10:42.363 --> 00:10:44.780
If you come up to me and you
say your talk was quite good,

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:46.970
I'm going to be really
happy unless I find out

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.420
that you are from the UK.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:50.760
We're quite means
like me, you know.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.840
So this word quite in English
does not mean the same thing

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.290
in every culture.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:58.290
And so what does this
look like in code?

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:01.330
What it looks like to me is
similar functions or arguments

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:03.552
that behave differently.

00:11:03.552 --> 00:11:06.010
So by way of example, I want
to talk a little about support

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:06.912
vector machines.

00:11:06.912 --> 00:11:08.870
Not going to teach you
support vector machines.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.450
I'm going to do
this really quick.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.170
We are trying to find a line
to separate these two classes.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.240
We want that line
to push the classes

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.690
apart as much as possible.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:21.250
So we want those dotted lines to
be as far from the solid line.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:22.990
But as we push
the classes apart,

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.910
we end up with on
the right hand side

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:26.440
those points that
are misclassified

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.603
or that kind of fall
inside that margin.

00:11:29.603 --> 00:11:31.270
So when we fit a
support vector machine,

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.640
we're trying to minimize
that misclassification,

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:35.060
maximize that margin.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:36.610
This is the push and pull.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:40.480
And so as you would hope in the
implementation and tidymodels,

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.330
there is indeed a parameter
that is the cost of having

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.740
points fall inside of a margin.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.580
And in scikitlearn as you
would hope there is a parameter

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.350
C that has to do with
the cost of points

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:52.850
falling in the margin.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:55.860
But let's look at those
a little more closely.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.330
tidymodels version is the
cost of predicting wrong,

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.730
so the cost of a sample
landing in the margin

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.840
or on the wrong side.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:06.580
In scikitlearn I could learn.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.080
notice that it says, see, the
strength of the regularization

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.440
is inversely proportional to
see the regularization being

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:15.770
that penalized for
misclassification.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.220
So these work opposite.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:18.720
They're capturing
the same thing.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.053
They're capturing the same idea.

00:12:20.053 --> 00:12:21.092
Again, neither is wrong.

00:12:21.092 --> 00:12:23.300
But when my students sitting
next to each other using

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.118
different languages tried the
same values for cost and for

00:12:26.118 --> 00:12:28.160
see because they were
trying to tune their model,

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.860
they got different results and
that was very confusing to them

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.120
and to me for quite some time.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.940
And so what happens here is that
then the students, of course,

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:38.990
conceptualize that
penalty differently.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.410
So the tidymodels
students are conceptualizing it

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.780
as a penalty on
misclassification,

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:47.110
whereas the psychic students
are conceptualizing it

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.080
as like a balancing parameter,
a regularization parameter.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:52.840
How much do we
balance the margin,

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:56.440
the desire for a wide margin
with the desire for less points

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.420
inside that margin?

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:01.880
So it's, you know, again, not
wrong leads to different ideas.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.230
And so what I would remind
everyone to do is, you know,

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.630
be explicit if you're
designing tools.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:09.550
I think cost of
misclassification

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.710
helped me track down
what was going on.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:15.130
I think amount of regularization
did not because regularization

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.700
is a little more general.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.800
And then, you
know, I don't think

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.690
I'm not asking for consistency
in parameterization.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.090
I'm not asking that you make
sure everything's the same.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.060
I'm asking you be upfront
because it's not possible

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:28.950
that everything's the
same, and people like me

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:33.280
need to be able to track
down what the difference is.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:35.560
My advice for educators
and practitioners,

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.013
state everything in words.

00:13:37.013 --> 00:13:38.680
Say things like when
the cost is higher,

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:41.230
we expect less
misclassification and the cost

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.420
of that is a smaller
margin, like if you say that

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:47.990
out loud, than you are
understanding that parameter.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:51.290
And then as always,
translate these choices

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:54.780
to non-code language.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:56.708
Term three moving
swiftly along, I think.

00:13:56.708 --> 00:13:58.000
But learning language is slang.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.400
Terms become hard.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.755
My favorite slang term
in English is stan.

00:14:01.755 --> 00:14:03.630
You know this phrase
stan, to stan something,

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.070
or to be a stan.

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:06.550
It means you're
like our big fan.

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:10.130
Basically, we're all
stands of are in here.

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:13.370
How many of where it came from?

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.770
So this came from a
music video and song

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:19.010
by Eminem about a very obsessed
fan whose name was literally

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:20.370
Stan like Stanley.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:21.950
And somehow over
the years, this word

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.560
evolved to mean
something much broader

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:25.940
and something that is used know.

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:27.523
And people don't
even remember that it

00:14:27.523 --> 00:14:30.300
came from a person's name.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.780
And so I think the
equivalent of slang in code

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:36.330
is maybe these helper
wrapper functions that

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.180
contain a whole lot of meaning,
like way more than what

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.100
that one word might suggest.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:46.370
And when I see this in my
class is in tuning parameters.

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:49.720
So when you tune, and we're
sticking here with those vector

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.270
machines, when
you tune, you need

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:54.320
to decide which values
you're going to try.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:58.120
Right and in both Python
on top and R on the bottom,

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:00.610
you can just sort
of manually create

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.530
kind of that list
of different values

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.200
that we're going to
try for our model.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.040
But tidy models also comes
with the shortcut where

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.117
we can access built in
functions with the names

00:15:13.117 --> 00:15:14.950
of the parameters, and
it will automatically

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:18.130
choose values for you that
it thinks are reasonable.

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:19.130
And like, I love this.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:20.327
I'm not saying this is bad.

00:15:20.327 --> 00:15:21.910
Like, I would much
rather have Max Kugn

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.110
decide which values I'm
tuning than me decide.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:24.910
That's great.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.450
Right I don't know why.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:31.970
Like 0.0009 and 32 are better
choices than ten, 20, 30.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:33.590
But I assume max does.

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:35.500
Or whoever wrote this
part of tidymodels.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.130
I'm fine with it.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:40.700
But again, this was my
biggest shock in the class.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:42.590
The Python students
picked it up better,

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:43.680
like everyone understood

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.420
tuning the first time
that I taught it.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.030
And then nine weeks of
hands on activities later.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:49.580
If you ask the
students about tuning,

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.355
they went into detail
on the functions.

00:15:51.355 --> 00:15:53.730
And if you ask the Python
students, they got the concept.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:57.110
We just try a bunch of different
values and see what sticks.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.495
And so that was
surprising to me.

00:15:58.495 --> 00:15:59.870
And so I don't
really have advice

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.555
like shortcut
functions are great.

00:16:01.555 --> 00:16:02.930
I don't think they
should change.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.100
I'm not saying that.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.980
I do think, again that
translating to English

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:13.410
is a good thing, but most
importantly for educators,

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:15.020
(not clicking for me)

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:17.010
There we go, for educators.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.080
Think about if you're
using a wrapper function,

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.705
basically assume that
what you're doing

00:16:20.705 --> 00:16:22.060
is glossing over that concept.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:24.570
So that's OK like I
do this with step k,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:26.520
I don't teach all of
pKa necessarily when

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:30.780
I'm pre-processing, but
avoid shortcut functions

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.155
on things that are actually
learning goals in your class.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:37.410
OK I got 2 minutes.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:41.010
I think the next one
I will do quickly.

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:43.340
So the last challenge
I want to point out

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:46.880
is like meaning that isn't
in words, that's in gestures

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:51.050
and in the movie Encanto we
have it's not moving this moment

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:52.363
where she points with her lips.

00:16:52.363 --> 00:16:53.780
And I've learned
that this is like

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.790
a super cool cultural
thing in Colombia

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:57.060
that we don't do in the US.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.740
Is pointing with your lips.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.030
So here's your pop quiz
and the code context.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:04.569
And I won't make you vote
because I'm low on time.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:07.480
(How is the question
is not showing?)

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.990
How does the k-means function

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:10.099
choose initial clusters?

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:12.010
If you don't know
k-means ignore it.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:14.180
I asked this on Twitter.

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:15.510
This is what people said.

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:19.190
This is what I would have
said before I dug into this.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:20.520
And it is not correct.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.069
So here is how I used
to teach k-means.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:25.869
Choose your initial
centroid by choosing

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:27.670
three random observations.

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:30.070
Assign all the points
to the nearest centroid,

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:32.510
re-compute the centroid
of each cluster,

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:34.240
keep going until
you have clusters.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:35.960
I've taught it
that way for years.

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:37.600
It turns out that
nothing about that

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:40.510
is correct about how k-means 
actually works.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:42.260
I mean, it can set it to be.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:44.590
But that is not the
default in this function.

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.470
The default in this
function, although it

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:51.700
says that it will choose three
initial observations down

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:53.260
there, that method
Hartigan-Wong,

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:55.250
default method. Hartigan-Wang

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:57.910
neither initializes that
way, nor does it even

00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.960
update in the same way
as the other methods.

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:03.775
So everything I've just
told you is not the default.

00:18:03.775 --> 00:18:05.650
In scikitlearn they're
a little more upfront.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:11.285
But now this initialization k
plus plus, that's different.

00:18:11.285 --> 00:18:12.660
At least they kind
of explain it,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.260
but they just say it's smart.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:18.620
And then you can think about
using different functions in R

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:22.250
that one contains k plus plus
k plus plus is not an option

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:25.190
to the base k means function.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:28.670
But now we don't have an
option to choose your iteration

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:29.660
process.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.330
So there is literally
no way to guarantee,

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.040
at least with these
two implementations,

00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:37.800
that the R and the
Python output matches.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.360
And so what was the result?

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:40.348
Just frustration.

00:18:40.348 --> 00:18:42.390
Just you cannot make that
output be the same with

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:45.340
the implementations people use.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:46.802
And so I think
these get repeated.

00:18:46.802 --> 00:18:48.760
I'm going to skip to kind
of my final takeaways

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:50.770
because they contain those ones.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:52.520
So recap.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:53.750
We have these challenges.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:56.470
And these challenges that
you see in language also

00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:57.730
appear in coding.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:00.520
So the developers in the
room, what I want you to do,

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.920
don't assume that the
choice of the parameter name

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:05.460
tells people what
that parameter does.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.290
Don't assume that
these default choices

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:08.483
are the obvious choices.

00:19:08.483 --> 00:19:09.400
They're not universal.

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:11.620
You have to document them.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:16.110
And the order that we code
is the order that we think.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.360
For practitioners
again, translation

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.170
is not going to be a
direct word for word.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.060
Please discuss what
you're deciding.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.140
Please document.

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:25.360
Please use text.

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:28.150
And for educators, really
what you want to do

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:32.683
is force your students to be
explicit about every decision.

